research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-347-## 5. What is taken up later in the reading order
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-348-
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-349-Stated as reading order, and as no claim at all about what this library
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-350-currently proves.
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-351-
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:352:- **Higher derivatives, and Taylor's theorem with the integral remainder.** The
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-353-  integral remainder is an application of [[thm-integration-by-parts]] and needs
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:354:  derivatives of order $n$. The later Darboux/L'Hopital/Taylor page proves the
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-355-  Peano, Lagrange, Cauchy and Schlomilch-Roche forms but explicitly excludes the
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-356-  integral remainder. It is therefore absent from the current library, with no
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-357-  later published page assigned to it; this is a statement about the present
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md:358:  reading order, not a theorem about Taylor remainders.
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-359-- **Bounded variation and the Riemann-Stieltjes integral.** The integrator
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-360-  $\mathrm{d}\alpha$ generalises everything on this page, and the sharpened
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-361-  second mean value theorem belongs with it. That is a later page of the same
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-362-  track, and it is not built.
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-363-- **Improper integrals.** $\int_0^{\infty} f$ is not defined anywhere in this
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-364-  library at this point in the reading order, which is why
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-365-  [[thm-integral-test-for-series]] concludes with the boundedness of the sequence
research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/rem-integral-conventions-and-scope.md-366-  $\bigl(\int_0^N f\bigr)_N$ instead. Identifying the two is what that later
