research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9182-+## Statement
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9183-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9184-+Let $n\ge1$. If there is a real $\delta>0$ such that $f$ is
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9185-+$n$-times differentiable on the open interval
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9186-+$N_\delta(a)=(a-\delta,a+\delta)$, then
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9187-+$$\frac{R_{n,a}f(x)}{(x-a)^n}\longrightarrow0\qquad(x\to a).$$
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9188-+Equivalently, in the usual little-$o$ shorthand,
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9189-+$f(x)=T_{n,a}f(x)+o((x-a)^n)$. For $n=0$, the analogous assertion is the
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9190-+separate continuity condition at $a$: for every $\varepsilon>0$, all
--
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9207-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9208-+1.1 Under the separate $n=0$ hypothesis, the quantified assertion is exactly the definition of continuity at $a$.  [given, L2]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9209-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9210-+1.2 For $n=1$, the derivative definition gives $$\frac{f(x)-f(a)-f'(a)(x-a)}{x-a} =\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}-f'(a)\longrightarrow0.$$ This is the required base case.  [base, given, L2]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9211-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9212-+1.3 Assume $n\ge2$ and the assertion through order $n-1$. Put $R=R_{n,a}f$. Then $R(a)=R'(a)=0$, and $R'(x)=R_{n-1,a}(f')(x)$.  [L1, algebra]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9213-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9214-+2.1 By the induction hypothesis applied to $f'$, $R'(x)/(x-a)^{n-1}\to0$. Applying the Cauchy quotient lemma to $R(x)-R(a)$ and $(x-a)^n$ gives $R(x)/(x-a)^n=R'(\xi)/(\iota(n)(\xi-a)^{n-1})$ for a point $\xi$ between $a$ and $x$.  [step 1.3, ih, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9215-+
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log-9216-+3.1 Since $\xi\to a$, the right side tends to $0$. This proves the Peano estimate without assuming continuity of $f^{(n)}$.  [step 2.1, L2, discharge-induction] ∎
