research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10064:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-276-1.2 For $n=1$, the derivative definition gives $$\frac{f(x)-f(a)-f'(a)(x-a)}{x-a} =\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}-f'(a)\longrightarrow0.$$ This is the required base case.  [base, given, L2]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10065:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-277-
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10066:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-278-1.3 Assume $n\ge2$ and the assertion through order $n-1$. Put $R=R_{n,a}f$. Then $R(a)=R'(a)=0$, and $R'(x)=R_{n-1,a}(f')(x)$.  [L1, algebra]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10067:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-279-
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10068:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-280-2.1 By the induction hypothesis applied to $f'$, $R'(x)/(x-a)^{n-1}\to0$. Applying the Cauchy quotient lemma to $R(x)-R(a)$ and $(x-a)^n$ gives $R(x)/(x-a)^n=R'(\xi)/(\iota(n)(\xi-a)^{n-1})$ for a point $\xi$ between $a$ and $x$.  [step 1.3, ih, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10069:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md-281-
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/certifier-a6-thm-taylor-peano-remainder.log:10070:research/audit/wave11-certifier-tasks/thm-taylor-peano-remainder.md:282:3.1 Since $\xi\to a$, the right side tends to $0$. This proves the Peano estimate without assuming continuity of $f^{(n)}$.  [step 2.1, L2, discharge-induction] ∎
